"slots": {
"question": [
{
"claim": "The paper investigates whether online political hostility is associated with political engagement and its antecedents, rather than solely with deprivation or personality-based explanations.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "In this article, we examine a different possibility. Classical research on political behavior emphasizes that education, internal efficacy, political interest, political knowledge, and participation are central to democratic engagement... We extend this revisionist perspective to online political hostility by asking whether hostile political expression on X/Twitter is associated not only with deprivation or personality-based explanations, but also with political engagement and its antecedents.",
"page": 2
}
]
}
],
"central_claim": [
{
"claim": "Online political hostility is positively associated with various indicators of political engagement and its antecedents, including education, internal efficacy, political interest, political knowledge, and participation.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "Online political hostility is positively associated with these indicators, with the strongest associations for political interest, political knowledge, and online participation.",
"page": 1
},
{
"text": "Building on classical theories of political engagement [17,19] and recent work on the darker sides of engagement, we find that online political hostility is positively associated with several indicators of political engagement and its antecedents.",
"page": 15
}
]
}
],
"evidence": [
{
"claim": "The study uses a dataset combining panel survey data with behavioral data from X/Twitter for American users, measuring hostility via a Super-Unsupervised word embeddings approach and engagement via self-reported surveys.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "Combining two years of survey and behavioral data from X/Twitter, we examine associations between online political hostility and indicators of political engagement and its antecedents among American users.",
"page": 1
},
{
"text": "Our key measure of online political hostility is based on a word embeddings approach termed the Super-Unsupervised (SU) approach... We combine these data with panel survey data on political engagement among American users.",
"page": 2
},
{
"text": "We rely on a data infrastructure combining panel survey data with online behavioral data from X... The total number of panelists where we were able to link survey responses to their X handle in this period was 2567.",
"page": 6
}
]
}
],
"key_findings": [
{
"claim": "There are positive and statistically significant associations between online political hostility and indicators of political engagement, with the strongest links found for political interest, political knowledge, and online participation.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "The largest associations are generally observed for the primary measure of political hostility rather than for the more general AFINN or Perspective API measures. The associations with online participation and political interest are, for example, 0.236... and 0.306... for online political hostility",
"page": 11
},
{
"text": "Online political hostility is positively associated with these indicators, with the strongest associations for political interest, political knowledge, and online participation.",
"page": 1
}
]
},
{
"claim": "The association between political engagement and online hostility is stronger among users with higher levels of leader-directed affective polarization, particularly for participatory indicators.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "The formal interaction models show the clearest support for online and offline participation, while the evidence is weaker or inconsistent for more distal antecedents such as education... The conditional pattern should therefore be treated as exploratory for the remaining indicators.",
"page": 14
},
{
"text": "The descriptive pattern suggests that the association between engagement and hostility is often stronger among more affectively polarized users... for those high on affective polarization fourteen out of eighteen are significant.",
"page": 13
}
]
},
{
"claim": "Politically hostile users are more active and have greater potential reach, posting more frequently and having more friends and followers.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "The count-model results indicate that higher political hostility is associated with more posts and with larger numbers of followers and, to a lesser extent, friends. A change from the mean level of hostility to 1.5 standard deviations above the mean is associated with an increase in the number of posts from around 420 to around 900 and an increase in the number of followers from around 220 to around 370.",
"page": 15
},
{
"text": "Engaged but hostile social media users do not simply cater to fringe audiences; they post more frequently and have more friends and followers.",
"page": 1
}
]
},
{
"claim": "Temporal within-person analyses using Random-Intercept Cross-Lagged Panel Models show that increases in online political engagement are followed by higher levels of online political hostility.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "In brief, within-person increases in online political engagement are followed by higher levels of online political hostility across the three hostility measures. We also observe some evidence of the reverse temporal association, in which hostility is followed by higher political engagement, although this pattern is weaker and less consistent.",
"page": 15
}
]
}
