"claim": "The paper disputes the view that online hostility is primarily driven by socioeconomic marginalization, status threat, or personality-based traits like sadism, arguing instead that it is also associated with normative democratic engagement.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "Existing explanations of online hostility can be grouped into at least two distinct literatures. One set of accounts emphasizes socioeconomic marginalization, status threat, and grievance... A second set of accounts emphasizes personality and emotion-regulation... In this article, we examine a different possibility.",
"page": 2
},
{
"text": "These findings suggest that online political hostility is not only associated with deprivation, marginalization, or personality-based explanations, as highlighted in prior work [14,15]. It is also associated with forms of engagement that are often considered central to democratic citizenship.",
